---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---
<BaseLayout
  title="Traditional Wear Trip Budget: Cost Breakdown"
  description="Detailed budget for a traditional clothing trip: Hanfu rentals, custom qipao, hotels, trains, food. 7-day and 14-day per-person cost estimates."
  canonicalUrl="/plan-trip/budget"
  articleDate="2026-08-03"
  breadcrumbs={{ name: "Home", url: "/" }, { name: "Plan Your Trip", url: "/plan-trip/" }, { name: "Budget Guide", url: "/plan-trip/budget/" }}>
  <section class="pt-24 pb-16"><div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
    <span class="text-xs font-medium text-[#b5343a] tracking-[0.15em] uppercase">Plan Your Trip · Budget</span>
    <h1 class="font-serif-sc text-3xl sm:text-4xl lg:text-5xl font-bold leading-tight tracking-tight text-stone-900 mt-3">Trip Budget: What Does It Cost?</h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-stone-500 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">Surprisingly affordable compared to Europe or Japan. Full per-person breakdown.</p>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-400 mt-3">All prices in Chinese Yuan (RMB). 1 USD \u2248 7.2 RMB, 1 EUR \u2248 7.9 RMB as of 2026. Budget tier assumes hostels/budget hotels + street food; comfortable tier assumes 3-4 star hotels + mid-range restaurants.</p>
    <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-14 mb-4">7-Day Beijing-Xi''an (per person, excl. flights)</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto"><table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse"><thead><tr class="border-b border-stone-200 text-left"><th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold">Category</th><th class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold">Budget</th><th class="py-3 font-semibold">Comfortable</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Hotels 6 nights</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">1,200-1,800</td><td class="py-2.5">2,400-3,600</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Train Beijing-Xi''an</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">515</td><td class="py-2.5">825</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">City transport</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">200-300</td><td class="py-2.5">400-500</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Meals 7 days</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">500-700</td><td class="py-2.5">1,000-1,400</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Hanfu rental x2</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">300-500</td><td class="py-2.5">600-800</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Tang suit</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">200-300</td><td class="py-2.5">500-800</td></tr><tr class="border-b border-stone-100"><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">Attractions</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4">300-400</td><td class="py-2.5">500-700</td></tr><tr><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 font-semibold">Total</td><td class="py-2.5 pr-4 font-semibold">3,400-4,900</td><td class="py-2.5 font-semibold">7,200-10,300</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
    <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-12 mb-4">14-Day Multi-City</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">Budget: 8,400-9,900 RMB (~1,160-1,370 USD). Comfortable: 15,400-18,300 RMB (~2,130-2,530 USD). Includes 5 train rides between Shanghai, Suzhou, Hangzhou, Nanjing, Xi''an, and Chengdu.</p>
    <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-12 mb-4">How to Save Money</h2>
    <ul class="space-y-2 text-stone-600">
      <li><strong>Book trains early:</strong> High-speed tickets go on sale 15 days ahead and sell out for popular routes. Book via Trip.com or the official 12306.cn (Chinese-only, but Trip.com mirrors it in English).</li>
      <li><strong>Use Meituan or Xiaohongshu for Hanfu bookings:</strong> These Chinese apps typically offer 20-30% off walk-in rental prices. Search "\u6c49\u670d\u4f53\u9a8c" + city name and book through the app.</li>
      <li><strong>Stay near metro stations:</strong> Hotels in Beijing and Shanghai one or two metro stops from tourist centers are often 50% cheaper than those right at the landmarks. The metro system is excellent.</li>
      <li><strong>Eat local:</strong> A filling bowl of Lanzhou lamian (hand-pulled noodles) costs 15-25 RMB. Street breakfast (jianbing crepes, baozi) runs 5-15 RMB. Skip hotel breakfasts.</li>
      <li><strong>Group tickets:</strong> If traveling with 3+ people, many Hanfu shops offer group discounts. Similarly, some museums have group rates for 5+ visitors.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2 class="font-serif-sc text-2xl font-bold text-stone-900 mt-12 mb-4">Payment Tips</h2>
    <p class="text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">China is nearly cashless. Download Alipay or WeChat Pay before your trip and link an international credit card — both now support foreign cards for tourist accounts. Carry 500-1000 RMB in cash as backup for small street vendors. ATMs accepting foreign cards are widely available at airports and major bank branches (ICBC, Bank of China).</p>
    <p class="text-sm text-stone-400 mt-3">Tip: Set up Alipay''s TourPass or TourCard feature — it creates a prepaid digital wallet you can top up with your home bank card, avoiding per-transaction foreign exchange fees.</p>
    <div class="mt-8"><a href="/plan-trip" class="text-sm text-stone-400 hover:text-stone-600 transition-colors">Back to Plan Your Trip</a></div>
  </div></section>
</BaseLayout>
